WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:06.230
What if I told you I knew a way to dodge taxes
on a chunk of your paycheck? Or how about

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:10.990
an investment opportunity that will never
pay a penny of taxes on the growth no matter

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:14.170
how big it gets? Sound too good to be true?

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:18.590
No, we’re not talking about some off-shore
account in the Cayman islands. This is the

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:24.920
amazing tax shelter of the IRA - a government-created
program designed to incentivize you to save

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.040
more for your future self.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:41.000
In 1974, Congress wanted a way to incentivize
the country to start saving for their own

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:45.489
retirement, and rely less on government programs
or company pensions. Their result was the

00:00:45.489 --> 00:00:47.589
creation of the first IRA.

00:00:47.589 --> 00:00:54.010
The “I” stands for “Individual”, meaning
you don’t get to co-own an IRA with someone

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:59.210
else, and it’s not dependent on a company
offering it to you, like a 401(k). The “R”

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:03.060
stands for retirement, meaning you promise
not to withdraw the money until you reach

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:08.470
a certain age. And the “A” can either
mean “account” or “arrangement” according

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:14.520
to the IRS. Contrary to popular belief, an
IRA isn’t an “investment” itself with

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:20.150
a set interest rate or statistics. It’s
a holding cell for your investment of choice.

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:24.650
An Individual Retirement “Account” can
be set up at nearly any bank or investment

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:29.860
company, and can hold investments like mutual
funds, stocks or ETFs. But an IRA doesn’t

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:34.920
have be an “account”. “Individual Retirement
Arrangements” allow you to apply the same

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:40.280
tax-benefits to an investment like a piece
of land or a small business. Some folks have

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:45.640
even used this “arrangement” to tax-shelter
dairy cows, a car wash and a habanero pepper

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:50.130
farm! As long as you play by the rules, you
can get pretty creative about what you choose

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:51.350
to invest in.

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:56.450
IRA’s can be broken down to two primary
styles; “Traditional” and “Roth”.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:01.310
The Traditional IRA is the O.G. IRA. When
you’re going “Traditional”, you’re

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:06.770
allowed to save up to $6,000 dollars a year,
or $7,000 dollars if you’re over age 50.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:12.670
And as long as you meet the criteria, any
money you save into a Traditional IRA is tax-deductible.

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:17.260
Translation: The more you save, the less taxes
you’ll owe this year!

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:22.440
And since you’re paying less taxes, you
have more money to…potentially invest in

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:27.010
your IRA! Essentially, it helps you boost
up the amount you save every year. As the

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:32.110
years go by and your account grows, you don’t
have to report the growth on your taxes. But

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:36.220
when retirement arrives and it’s time to
pull to the money out, the amount you withdraw

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:39.030
gets taxed as ordinary income.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:43.680
If you’re a freelancer or own a small business,
you qualify for some special variations of

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:50.850
the Traditional IRA; the first is the SEP
IRA. SEP stands for Simplified Employee Pension,

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:56.400
and can be ideal for self-employed people.
The SEP will allow you to raise your $6,000

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:02.220
dollar funding limit to the lesser of $56,000
dollars or 25% of your compensation. That’s

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:09.610
a HUGE potential tax deduction each year!
But beware, if you have any employees, you’ll

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:13.330
probably have to fund their IRA’s at the
same rate as your own.

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:18.770
And if you work for or own a small business,
the SIMPLE IRA is kind of like a Traditional

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:25.260
IRA blended with a 401(k). Available to companies
with 100 employees or less, the $6,000 dollar

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:31.130
funding limit is raised to $13,000 dollars
and offers a saving-match incentive to workers,

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:36.940
usually a 3% matching contribution. So whether
you own or work for the small business, the

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:41.780
SEP and SIMPLE can help you level up a basic
Traditional IRA

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:47.739
Then there’s the Roth IRA, the cooler, younger
brother of the Traditional IRA. Making its

00:03:47.739 --> 00:03:54.920
debut in the 1990’s, and named after Senator
Bill Roth, the Roth IRA flips the tax benefit.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:59.730
Instead of getting a tax deduction on the
income you put in now, the capital gains,

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:05.891
interest, and growth can be withdrawn tax-free
in your golden years — with no limit! This

00:04:05.891 --> 00:04:10.580
is super attractive to younger investors,
since their investments can rack up so much

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:15.870
compound-growth over their lifetime, there’s
a potential boatload of taxes they can avoid.

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:21.709
Let’s say you started maxing out your Roth
IRA at $6,000 dollars a year every year starting

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:27.110
at age 25. And let’s imagine that money
gets invested in index funds that grow at

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:34.110
average rate of 8% per year. If you did just
that until you reached 65, you’d have around

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:39.800
$1.67 Million dollars.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:44.570
Under ordinary circumstances, whenever you
profit from an investment, you need to pay

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:49.250
taxes. But thanks to your “arrangement”
with the government, your Roth IRA hooks you

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:56.490
up big time. In our simplified example, your
“gains” account for $1.43 of your $1.67

00:04:56.490 --> 00:05:01.510
million dollars. And assuming you owed a “15%
capital gains tax” on those gains, you’d

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:07.830
normally be on the hook for over $214,000
dollars in taxes. But thanks to the Roth,

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:10.130
that number is $0!

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:14.770
A couple important things to keep in mind
when investing through an IRA. First, you

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:21.310
can’t touch the money until age 59 ½, or
you’ll with get hit with taxes AND a 10%

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:25.840
early withdrawal penalty. Though there are
a few exceptions to this rule, like paying

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:31.979
for college or buying a home. And contributions
or deductions might be limited or eliminated

00:05:31.979 --> 00:05:36.520
if you and/or your spouse aren’t working
or have a 401(k) or other retirement plan

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:42.270
through your job. And even non-working spouses
can save into their IRA if they choose.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:47.090
So which IRA is right for you? Luckily, you
don’t have to limit yourself to only one

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:52.770
- many people have multiple IRAs—as long
as the total investment doesn’t exceed your

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:58.180
yearly cap. The factors that might make a
Roth, Traditional, SEP or SIMPLE IRA a good

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:03.030
fit are unique to you - your age, your income,
your goals.

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:07.360
Speaking with a tax professional and a financial
planner about your situation will always be

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:12.100
a safe bet when deciding which IRA to house
your investments of choice. They can help

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:16.690
you make sure you play by all the rules of
the “arrangement” and don’t overlook

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:17.690
anything.

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:22.790
But one thing’s for sure - the advantages
of saving into an IRA early and often are

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:25.290
truly too good to pass up.

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:26.480
And that’s our two cents!

